WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
Data processing must preserve trust boundaries after a request is accepted.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:11.480
SQL injection prevention depends on parameterized queries and safe ORM patterns that keep data separate from query structure.

3
00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:16.220
Similar thinking applies to NoSQL and other query construction.

4
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:23.520
Dynamic filters, sort fields, raw query fragments, and user-controlled expressions should be reviewed carefully.

5
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:30.020
The goal is not to demonstrate payloads, but to prevent untrusted input from becoming executable query logic.

6
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:36.380
Command execution, path handling, and file operations require cautious design.

7
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.880
APIs should avoid letting request data control commands, interpreters, or filesystem locations.

8
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:52.900
File uploads need size limits, content expectations, safe naming, constrained storage, cleanup, and authorization around access or deletion.

9
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.680
A filename, extension, content type, or metadata field is not proof that the file is safe for storage or downstream processing.

10
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:08.960
Serialization formats such as JSON, YAML, XML, and CSV are useful, but parser choice and limits matter.

11
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:19.700
Unsafe deserialization at a high level is the risk of turning untrusted data into behavior, objects, references, or resource use the application did not intend.

12
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:28.200
Safer processing uses constrained parser modes, schemas, size and depth limits, and clear decisions about what fields can influence server-side behavior.

13
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:34.400
Webhooks are security-sensitive because they allow external systems to trigger application behavior.

14
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:42.340
A secure webhook design validates source, integrity, freshness, expected event type, and intended purpose.

15
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:47.640
Request signing concepts and replay resistance concepts can help when used correctly.

16
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:53.180
The API should also handle duplicate delivery, delayed delivery, and malformed events safely.

17
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:59.600
Webhooks are not just inbound requests; they are delegated automation paths that deserve monitoring and review.
